>> B2: Describe how file descriptors are associated with open files.
>> Are file descriptors unique within the entire OS or just within a
>> single process?

open() calls filesys_open(), allocates a file_desc with fd = next_fd++
and appends it to the thread's list.  Other calls look the fd up with
find_fd().  fd 0 and 1 are never handed out; they are keyboard and
console.

Descriptors are unique only within a process: two processes may both
have fd 2 for different files, and fds are not inherited.  Opening the
same file twice gives two fds with independent struct file and
position.

---- ALGORITHMS ----

>> B3: Describe your code for reading and writing user data from the
>> kernel.

"Check first, then dereference":

  - valid_range(ptr, size): every byte must be non-NULL, below
    PHYS_BASE (is_user_vaddr) and mapped (pagedir_get_page != NULL).
  - argument(f, i): reads the i-th word above the user esp after
    validating its 4 bytes; the syscall number is argument 0.
  - copy_string(): copies a user string byte by byte into a kernel
    page, validating each byte, until '\0'.  File names and exec
    command lines are always copied before use.
  - read/write: the whole buffer is validated with valid_range(), then
    file_read/file_write/putbuf use the user pointer directly.

Any failure calls terminate_bad_user(): exit_status = -1, thread_exit().

>> B4: Suppose a system call causes a full page (4,096 bytes) of data
>> to be copied from user space into the kernel.  What is the least
>> and the greatest possible number of inspections of the page table
>> (e.g. calls to pagedir_get_page()) that might result?  What about
>> for a system call that only copies 2 bytes of data?  Is there room
>> for improvement in these numbers, and how much?

Ours: one pagedir_get_page() per byte, so a full page costs 4,096
inspections (fewer only if it stops early on a bad byte) and 2 bytes
cost 2.

Minimum possible: one per distinct page touched.  A full page needs 1
if page-aligned, 2 otherwise; 2 bytes need 1, or 2 if they straddle a
page boundary.

Improvement: checking per page instead of per byte reduces 4,096 to at
most 2.  Checking only ptr < PHYS_BASE and letting page_fault() catch
unmapped pages (get_user/put_user) needs zero inspections in the
common case.  We kept the per-byte version for simplicity and obvious
correctness; speed does not matter here.

>> B5: Briefly describe your implementation of the "wait" system call
>> and how it interacts with process termination.

wait(pid) -> process_wait(pid):

  1. Search the current thread's children list for pid.  Not found
     (not my child) or waited already true -> return -1.
  2. Set waited = true; sema_down(&wait_sema).
  3. On wake, read exit_status, remove the record from the list, call
     release_child(record, parent), return the status.

In process_exit() the child stores exit_status into its child_record,
sema_up(&wait_sema), then release_child(record, child).  If the child
exited first, wait_sema is already 1 and sema_down returns at once.
A kernel-killed process keeps the default exit_status = -1.

>> B6: Any access to user program memory at a user-specified address
>> can fail due to a bad pointer value.  Such accesses must cause the
>> process to be terminated.  System calls are fraught with such
>> accesses, e.g. a "write" system call requires reading the system
>> call number from the user stack, then each of the call's three
>> arguments, then an arbitrary amount of user memory, and any of
>> these can fail at any point.  This poses a design and
>> error-handling problem: how do you best avoid obscuring the primary
>> function of code in a morass of error-handling?  Furthermore, when
>> an error is detected, how do you ensure that all temporarily
>> allocated resources (locks, buffers, etc.) are freed?  In a few
>> paragraphs, describe the strategy or strategies you adopted for
>> managing these issues.  Give an example.

1. Checks live in small helpers.  argument(), valid_range() and
   copy_string() do all validation and kill the process themselves, so
   syscall code just writes "fd = argument (f, 1)" with no error path.

2. Validate before acquiring resources.  Each syscall reads and checks
   all arguments and buffers before taking filesys_lock, so a process
   is never killed while holding the lock.  Temporary kernel pages
   (file names) are freed on the failure path before
   terminate_bad_user().

3. Single cleanup point.  process_exit() runs however the process
   dies (exit(), bad pointer, exception) and closes all fds, re-allows
   writes to the executable, releases child records and destroys the
   page directory.

Example, write(fd, buffer, size): argument(f, 1..3) validate the user
stack; valid_range(buffer, size) validates the buffer; both exit(-1)
on failure with nothing held.  Only then: fd 1 -> putbuf(); otherwise
find_fd(), acquire filesys_lock, file_write(), release.

---- SYNCHRONIZATION ----

>> B7: The "exec" system call returns -1 if loading the new executable
>> fails, so it cannot return before the new executable has completed
>> loading.  How does your code ensure this?  How is the load
>> success/failure status passed back to the thread that calls "exec"?

process_execute() creates child_info (load_sema = 0), passes it to the
new thread and blocks on sema_down(&load_sema).  start_process() runs
load(), stores the result in child->load_success, then
sema_up(&load_sema).  The parent wakes, reads load_success and returns
tid or TID_ERROR (-1).  The record is on the heap and in the parent's
list before the child starts, so it stays valid even if the child
exits immediately.

>> B8: Consider parent process P with child process C.  How do you
>> ensure proper synchronization and avoid race conditions when P
>> calls wait(C) before C exits?  After C exits?  How do you ensure
>> that all resources are freed in each case?  How about when P
>> terminates without waiting, before C exits?  After C exits?  Are
>> there any special cases?

child_info has two owners.  release_child() clears the caller's alive
flag and frees the record if both are now false; the flag update and
test run with interrupts off, so P and C can neither both free nor
both skip freeing it.

  - P waits before C exits: P blocks on wait_sema.  C writes status,
    ups it, releases.  P wakes, reads status, releases -> freed.
  - P waits after C exits: status already stored, sema already 1; P
    returns at once and frees.
  - P exits first, no wait: process_exit() releases P's side of every
    child record.  C runs on; its release frees the record.
  - P exits after C, no wait: C already released; P's release frees.

C's struct thread is always freed by the scheduler, independently.
Special cases: double wait or non-child -> -1 (waited flag, per-process
list); failed load -> C exits normally, P drops its ownership in
process_execute(), record freed.

---- RATIONALE ----

>> B9: Why did you choose to implement access to user memory from the
>> kernel in the way that you did?

Check-then-dereference is the simpler documented method: no changes to
page_fault(), no inline assembly, and easy to reason about since a
pointer is never dereferenced before it is validated.  The cost is
speed (B4), which is irrelevant for this project.

>> B10: What advantages or disadvantages can you see to your design
>> for file descriptors?

Advantages: simple per-process list, no fixed open-file limit, memory
used only for open files, straightforward cleanup at exit.

Disadvantages: O(n) lookup and fds never reused (next_fd only grows).
An array indexed by fd would give O(1) lookup and number reuse.

>> B11: The default tid_t to pid_t mapping is the identity mapping.
>> If you changed it, what advantages are there to your approach?

Unchanged: pid == tid.  Each process is exactly one thread, so a
separate pid would add code for no benefit.  A different mapping would
only help if a process could have multiple threads.

